<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anton Bruckner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Anton Bruckner</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anton Bruckner was an Austrian composer and organist, born at Ansfelden near Linz, known for his symphonies and sacred music, including Masses, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> and motets. He worked as a teacher and organist at Sankt Florian, studied with the theorist Simon Sechter, and in 1868 took over Sechter's post teaching music theory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> Conservatory. His long symphonies, with their rich harmony, strong polyphony and unprepared modulations, are regarded as emblematic of the final stage of Austro-German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>. Critics led by Eduard Hanslick attacked their size and repetition, and he revised many of his works, leaving several versions; he died in Vienna in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner.jpg" alt="Anton Bruckner" width="330" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Anton_Bruckner_(1868).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…emblem of Austria and its monarchy: Beethoven in a work celebrating the end of the Napoleonic Wars, Rossini, Paganini, Donizetti, Clara Schumann, Smetana and <b>Bruckner</b>, while Bartók used it in his symphonic poem Kossuth (1903) as the sign of the Austrian enemy of the Hungarian Revolution of 1848.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a></li><li><blockquote>Composers have set it since the Renaissance, among them Monteverdi in his Vespers of 1610, Vivaldi, Johann Sebastian Bach (1723, revised 1733), <b>Bruckner</b>, Rachmaninoff and Arvo Pärt.</blockquote><a href="/songs/magnificat">Magnificat</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Anton Bruckner</b>'s first composition set the hymn's first strophe.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a></li><li><blockquote>The hymn was first sung to a Gregorian chant melody, and many composers have set it since: Lully, who was fatally injured while conducting his setting for the court of Louis XIV, Charpentier, Handel, Haydn, Mozart, Berlioz, Verdi, <b>Bruckner</b>, Dvořák and Britten among them.</blockquote><a href="/songs/te-deum">Te Deum</a></li><li><blockquote>…tune for four centuries: Jehan Titelouze (1623), Marc-Antoine Charpentier (five settings), Johann Pachelbel, Nicolas de Grigny, Johann Sebastian Bach (the chorale BWV 370 and the organ preludes BWV 631 and 667), Berlioz, Franck, <b>Bruckner</b>, Gustav Mahler, who set the Latin text in Part I of his Symphony No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li></ul></section>
